Example of the base prediction method that uses expected point differential against an "average" team adjusted for strength of schedule,
and with added bonus for # of top 10 and top 25 teams beat.

Step 1: Preparing data
```{r}
install.packages("readxl")   #install package needed to read excel files
library(readxl)

#create datasets for each side of the ball
Offense<- read_excel("Week 1 Stats/Offense Week 1.xlsx") 
Defense<- read_excel("Week 1 Stats/Defense Week 1.xlsx") 
ST<- read_excel("Week 1 Stats/Special Teams Week 1.xlsx") #read stats into datasets

df_list <- list(Offense, Defense, ST)
Week1 <- df_list %>% reduce(full_join, by = "School")  #join datasets to create week 1 stats database
Scedule1 <- read_excel("CFB Schedule.xlsx", sheet = "week 1 schedule")
Schedule1$School <- sub("^\\(\\d+\\)\\s*", "", Schedule1$Winner)    #remove ranking values from the name of the team
Schedule1$School <- sub("^\\(\\d+\\)\\s*", "", Schedule1$Loser)
Schedule1$Home <- trimws(Schedule1$Home)    #trim whitespace for all values, so that any extra spaces accidentally added are removed
Schedule1$Away <- trimws(Schedule1$Away)
Power_rankings$School <- trimws(
  Power_rankings$School
)
```

Create Prediction method
```
Predict_Game<- function(SchoolA, SchoolB){
  if (!(SchoolA %in% Power_rankings$School)){
    return(-1)
  }
  if (!(SchoolB %in% Power_rankings$School)){
    return(1)
  } 
  teamA<- Power_rankings[Power_rankings$School == SchoolA,][1,]
  teamB<- Power_rankings[Power_rankings$School == SchoolB,][1,]

